{
 "_comment": "DROP THE ATLAS GATE, and close the one missing cell in the decomposition.\n\nA. GATE REMOVAL AT THE 400-EPOCH PROTOCOL.\n   The gate is inert on every dataset it has been measured on: Photo +0.0003\n   (p=1.00), Yelp +0.0005 (p=0.55), Computers -0.0012 (p=1.00), CS +0.0000\n   (p=0.32). Four datasets, no effect, and `L_lean` equals `C_nopl` to four\n   decimals on both Computers and CS - the gate adds nothing even on top of\n   pseudo-labelling.\n\n   It is not free. On Photo the gate is 95,617 of 209,730 parameters - 46% of\n   the model - so removing it takes the efficiency ratio against DEMO from\n   0.289x to 0.157x. On Yelp it is 4,353 of 27,202 (16%), 0.795x to 0.668x.\n   Reporting a smaller model that scores the same is a stronger result than\n   reporting a larger one with a component we would have to defend as inert.\n\n   Yelp already has C_nogate at n=10 and yelp is natively 400 epochs, so its\n   cell is done and is not re-run here. ogbn-mag is deliberately EXCLUDED: 15\n   rotations x 5 seeds x 5.5 h is 412 GPU-hours, and the gate has never shown\n   an effect anywhere. It stays on the measured configuration with a footnote\n   rather than consuming three days to confirm a fifth null.\n\nB. C_nopl ON PHOTO (10 jobs).\n   Never run, and its absence caused a real error: comparing L_lean against\n   C_nogate alone, with no C_nopl to decompose it, made a simple\n   pseudo-labelling effect look like a gate-PL interaction. That reading was\n   reported and then withdrawn when Computers and CS showed L_lean = C_nopl\n   exactly. Photo is the only dataset where the decomposition still cannot be\n   checked. At 200 epochs to match the A_main base the other Photo ablations\n   use.\n\nCOST, measured from existing shards: photo 14.9 min/rot, computers 23.5,\ncs 77.2, arxiv ~45. 180 jobs, ~145 GPU-hours, ~30 h wall on five cards.",
